**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated, both objectives):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~936 bar / 0.27 (run1, single specimen, not reproduced)
- Balanced: ~658 bar / 0.31 (run6, clean, single specimen)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~421 bar / 0.44 (run3, minor wrinkles)

### Validation Status

- **17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s run1** (936 bar, 0.27): Single specimen, only 1 fit survived, SD=0, high apparent gain — **requires validation**. Runs 2 and 6 at same conditions gave 675 and 659 bar respectively with pore fractions 0.30–0.31. Run1's 936 bar is a large outlier vs. the reproducible ~660–675 bar range. The reproducible mean is ~660–675 bar / 0.30–0.31.
- **18-0add-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s** (runs 1–5): Multiple runs show pore fraction 0.32–0.44, modulus 339–468 bar. Run3 (0.44 PF, 421 bar) is the highest PF observed with reasonable modulus. This condition has 5 runs with variable results (high PF variability). Not a clean Pareto improvement over the 17p5-18deg cluster.

### Key Observations

The data shows a clear trade-off: conditions favoring high pore fraction (longer wait, no N2, warmer bath, lower polymer) tend to reduce modulus. The 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s cluster reproducibly gives ~660 bar / 0.30–0.31 — good modulus but modest pore fraction. The 18-15deg-600s-NoN2 cluster gives higher pore fraction (~0.36–0.44) but lower modulus (~340–470 bar).

**Under-explored region**: Intermediate bath temperatures (18–20°C) with polymer_wt ~17.5, moderate wait times (~75–120s), and **no nitrogen** have been sparsely tested. The `17p5-17deg-75s-NoN2-1800s` run1 gave 487 bar / 0.33 (clean membrane) — a potentially interesting balanced point. Run2 gave 397 bar / 0.37 (wrinkles). Run3 gave 428 bar / 0.35 (wrinkle). The no-N2 condition at bath_temp ~17–18°C with 17.5 wt% polymer shows elevated pore fraction (0.33–0.37) with moderate modulus.

**Gap**: bath_temp ~20–22°C with polymer_wt ~17.5, no nitrogen, short wait (~75s) has not been tested. Warmer bath slows phase separation, potentially increasing pore fraction while maintaining reasonable modulus. This is a genuinely under-sampled region that could shift the Pareto front.

**Recommendation**: Explore bath_temp = 21°C, polymer_wt = 17.5, no nitrogen, coupon_to_bath_wait_time = 75s, pullcast_speed = 10, mixing_temp = 65, nips_bath_wait_time = 1800s. This is adjacent to the 17°C no-N2 cluster (which showed 0.33–0.37 PF) but warmer, potentially pushing pore fraction higher while the 17.5 wt% polymer maintains reasonable modulus. It is genuinely novel (bath_temp=21 with no-N2 and 17.5 wt% not previously tested) and physically motivated.

